1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,841
On October 27, 2026, an eruption broke through the sea,

2
00:00:04,841 --> 00:00:08,886
just off a small Japanese island called Iwo Jima.

3
00:00:09,386 --> 00:00:11,820
Within three weeks, the island had grown.

4
00:00:11,820 --> 00:00:14,134
About fifty hectares of brand-new land.

5
00:00:14,134 --> 00:00:16,390
That's roughly ninety football fields.

6
00:00:16,890 --> 00:00:18,332
One volcano did that.

7
00:00:18,332 --> 00:00:21,767
So what if every volcano on Earth erupted at once?

8
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:28,126
First, what actually happened.

9
00:00:28,126 --> 00:00:31,753
Iwo Jima sits about 1,200 kilometers south of Tokyo,

10
00:00:31,753 --> 00:00:35,449
and the whole island is the top of an active volcano.

11
00:00:35,949 --> 00:00:41,023
In late October, seismic sensors picked up a sharp jump in activity.

12
00:00:41,023 --> 00:00:42,067
On October 27,

13
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:47,364
Japan's Meteorological Agency reported the first photos of an eruption,

14
00:00:47,364 --> 00:00:50,199
just off the island's southeast coast.

15
00:00:50,699 --> 00:00:55,482
NASA's Landsat 9 satellite had photographed that coast the day before.

16
00:00:55,482 --> 00:00:59,855
When the clouds finally cleared on November 11, it saw new land,

17
00:00:59,855 --> 00:01:02,383
still hot enough to glow in infrared,

18
00:01:02,383 --> 00:01:04,911
and a long streak of floating pumice.

19
00:01:05,411 --> 00:01:09,159
On November 16, Japan's Coast Guard confirmed it.

20
00:01:09,159 --> 00:01:13,367
The new land is joined to the island's southeast beach.

21
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:17,803
NASA estimates it makes Iwo Jima about 1.7 percent bigger.

22
00:01:18,303 --> 00:01:21,407
What's still unknown is how long it will last.

23
00:01:21,407 --> 00:01:25,119
Land made mostly of ash is loose, and waves chew it up.

24
00:01:25,119 --> 00:01:29,640
After an eruption here in 2023, the new land was gone within weeks.

25
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:34,750
NASA's Daniel Slayback expects this one to wash away within a few months too.

26
00:01:34,750 --> 00:01:37,204
But, he adds, we'll have to wait and see.

27
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:41,292
Now, the what-if.

28
00:01:41,292 --> 00:01:47,684
The US Geological Survey counts about 1,350 potentially active volcanoes on Earth.

29
00:01:47,684 --> 00:01:54,311
And that's not counting the long chains of volcanoes along ridges on the ocean floor.

30
00:01:54,811 --> 00:01:58,621
In a typical year, only about forty to fifty of them erupt.

31
00:01:58,621 --> 00:01:59,978
Less than one in ten.

32
00:02:00,478 --> 00:02:02,182
So here are our rules.

33
00:02:02,182 --> 00:02:05,126
At the same moment, all 1,350 wake up.

34
00:02:05,126 --> 00:02:07,683
Each one erupts in its own style.

35
00:02:07,683 --> 00:02:09,620
Some ooze rivers of lava.

36
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:10,627
Some explode.

37
00:02:11,127 --> 00:02:14,239
And for now, we'll ignore whether this is even possible.

38
00:02:14,239 --> 00:02:16,184
We'll come back to that at the end.

39
00:02:18,933 --> 00:02:22,361
In the first minute, seen from space, the planet lights up.

40
00:02:22,361 --> 00:02:23,232
But not evenly.

41
00:02:23,732 --> 00:02:25,187
The glow traces lines.

42
00:02:25,187 --> 00:02:30,412
That's because most volcanoes sit where the giant plates of Earth's crust meet.

43
00:02:30,412 --> 00:02:34,446
About 60 percent of active volcanoes are on plate boundaries.

44
00:02:34,946 --> 00:02:39,149
The brightest line of all wraps around the Pacific Ocean.

45
00:02:39,149 --> 00:02:40,771
It's the Ring of Fire.

46
00:02:40,771 --> 00:02:46,743
It runs for about 40,000 kilometers, and it's made up of more than 450 volcanoes.

47
00:02:47,243 --> 00:02:48,868
Iwo Jima is one of them.

48
00:02:48,868 --> 00:02:51,441
So are Mount Fuji, Mount Saint Helens,

49
00:02:51,441 --> 00:02:54,623
and the long chain of volcanoes down the Andes.

50
00:02:55,123 --> 00:02:57,785
Out in the Atlantic, Iceland glows too.

51
00:02:57,785 --> 00:03:02,084
In Africa, a line of volcanoes runs down the East African Rift.

52
00:03:02,084 --> 00:03:05,496
And Hawaii lights up in the middle of the Pacific.

53
00:03:05,996 --> 00:03:10,468
Meanwhile, under the sea, where most of Earth's volcanoes are hidden,

54
00:03:10,468 --> 00:03:13,644
the long volcanic ridges on the seafloor join in.

55
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:21,314
By the end of the first day, the biggest problem for most of the world isn't lava.

56
00:03:21,314 --> 00:03:21,853
It's ash.

57
00:03:22,353 --> 00:03:25,939
Volcanic ash isn't soft, like the ash in a fireplace.

58
00:03:25,939 --> 00:03:29,457
It's tiny bits of rock, minerals and volcanic glass.

59
00:03:29,457 --> 00:03:33,449
It's hard, it's abrasive, and it doesn't dissolve in water.

60
00:03:33,949 --> 00:03:35,611
And aircraft hate it.

61
00:03:35,611 --> 00:03:36,245
In 1982,

62
00:03:36,245 --> 00:03:41,548
a British Airways jumbo jet flew into an ash cloud from Galunggung,

63
00:03:41,548 --> 00:03:43,369
a volcano in Indonesia.

64
00:03:43,369 --> 00:03:45,348
All four engines stopped.

65
00:03:45,848 --> 00:03:49,100
The jet dropped about seven and a half kilometers.

66
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:52,938
Then, in clearer air, the crew got the engines going again,

67
00:03:52,938 --> 00:03:54,824
and landed safely in Jakarta.

68
00:03:55,324 --> 00:03:58,288
Today, pilots stay away from ash completely.

69
00:03:58,288 --> 00:04:03,340
In April 2010, one Icelandic volcano spread ash across the skies of Europe.

70
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:07,652
Over a hundred thousand flights were cancelled in a single week,

71
00:04:07,652 --> 00:04:10,683
and about seven million travelers were stuck.

72
00:04:11,183 --> 00:04:12,520
That was one volcano.

73
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:16,341
With ash drifting downwind of hundreds of volcanoes at once,

74
00:04:16,341 --> 00:04:19,844
you can expect most of the world's air traffic to stop.

75
00:04:22,600 --> 00:04:25,523
Ash falls out of the sky within days to weeks.

76
00:04:25,523 --> 00:04:28,065
The bigger, longer problem is invisible.

77
00:04:28,065 --> 00:04:28,828
It's sulfur.

78
00:04:29,328 --> 00:04:34,593
Big explosive eruptions fire sulfur dioxide gas high into the stratosphere,

79
00:04:34,593 --> 00:04:35,857
above the weather.

80
00:04:35,857 --> 00:04:41,684
There, it turns into a haze of tiny droplets that reflect sunlight back into space.

81
00:04:42,184 --> 00:04:42,766
In 1991,

82
00:04:42,766 --> 00:04:49,965
Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines blasted columns of ash and gas up to about forty kilometers high,

83
00:04:49,965 --> 00:04:54,401
along with around seventeen million tonnes of sulfur dioxide.

84
00:04:54,901 --> 00:04:57,889
That haze spread around the Earth in about three weeks.

85
00:04:57,889 --> 00:05:00,170
Within a year, it covered the whole globe.

86
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:03,627
And the height record is even more recent.

87
00:05:03,627 --> 00:05:04,754
In January 2022,

88
00:05:04,754 --> 00:05:10,528
the underwater Hunga Tonga volcano sent its plume about fifty-eight kilometers up,

89
00:05:10,528 --> 00:05:11,936
into the mesosphere.

90
00:05:11,936 --> 00:05:14,893
The highest plume in the satellite record.

91
00:05:15,393 --> 00:05:20,199
Now picture hundreds of plumes like that, all at once, all feeding the same haze.

92
00:05:22,933 --> 00:05:25,779
Before we get to the coldest part, a quick favour.

93
00:05:25,779 --> 00:05:28,511
If you're enjoying this, hit like and subscribe.

94
00:05:28,511 --> 00:05:30,104
It really helps the channel.

95
00:05:30,604 --> 00:05:33,139
One year in, the sky is pale and milky.

96
00:05:33,139 --> 00:05:34,244
Sunsets glow red.

97
00:05:34,244 --> 00:05:35,934
And the planet is cooling.

98
00:05:36,434 --> 00:05:40,982
We know this works, because it has happened before, with just one volcano.

99
00:05:40,982 --> 00:05:41,904
After Pinatubo,

100
00:05:41,904 --> 00:05:46,635
the haze cooled the Northern Hemisphere by up to about half a degree Celsius.

101
00:05:47,135 --> 00:05:47,754
In 1815,

102
00:05:47,754 --> 00:05:53,942
Mount Tambora in Indonesia produced the largest volcanic eruption ever recorded.

103
00:05:54,442 --> 00:05:58,858
The next year, 1816, became known as the year without a summer.

104
00:05:58,858 --> 00:06:01,171
Snow fell in New England in July.

105
00:06:01,171 --> 00:06:06,497
Crops failed across much of North America, Western Europe and parts of Asia.

106
00:06:06,997 --> 00:06:09,563
Now add every volcano on Earth at once.

107
00:06:09,563 --> 00:06:13,248
Nobody can put an exact number on how cold it would get.

108
00:06:13,248 --> 00:06:17,853
But scientists who have looked at this scenario describe darker skies,

109
00:06:17,853 --> 00:06:21,866
a sharp drop in temperature, and plants struggling for light.

110
00:06:21,866 --> 00:06:25,419
A volcanic winter, worse than any in recorded history.

111
00:06:25,919 --> 00:06:29,135
The good news is that the haze doesn't last forever.

112
00:06:29,135 --> 00:06:32,721
After Pinatubo, the cooling faded after about three years,

113
00:06:32,721 --> 00:06:35,936
as the droplets slowly fell out of the stratosphere.

114
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:41,127
So a few years later, the sky clears.

115
00:06:41,127 --> 00:06:44,014
But volcanoes don't only breathe out sulfur.

116
00:06:44,014 --> 00:06:46,442
They also breathe out carbon dioxide.

117
00:06:46,442 --> 00:06:48,869
So, do we swap a freezer for an oven?

118
00:06:49,369 --> 00:06:50,581
Here's a surprise.

119
00:06:50,581 --> 00:06:55,764
You may have heard that volcanoes release more carbon dioxide than humans do.

120
00:06:55,764 --> 00:06:56,504
They don't.

121
00:06:56,504 --> 00:06:57,514
Not even close.

122
00:06:58,014 --> 00:07:01,504
All the world's volcanoes, on land and under the sea,

123
00:07:01,504 --> 00:07:04,994
add up to roughly a third of a billion tonnes a year.

124
00:07:04,994 --> 00:07:07,628
Humans emit around forty billion tonnes.

125
00:07:08,128 --> 00:07:13,540
That means we emit more than sixty times as much as every volcano on Earth combined.

126
00:07:14,040 --> 00:07:17,064
Even Pinatubo, at the height of its eruption,

127
00:07:17,064 --> 00:07:21,566
only matched humanity's carbon dioxide output for about nine hours.

128
00:07:22,066 --> 00:07:26,556
So a single burst, even a huge one, isn't the real carbon danger.

129
00:07:26,556 --> 00:07:31,392
The danger comes when eruptions keep going for a very, very long time.

130
00:07:31,392 --> 00:07:32,635
Hold that thought.

131
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:38,639
Of course, volcanoes aren't all the same size.

132
00:07:38,639 --> 00:07:40,400
A few are supervolcanoes,

133
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:45,892
able to blast out more than a thousand cubic kilometers of material in one go.

134
00:07:46,392 --> 00:07:47,386
By our rough math,

135
00:07:47,386 --> 00:07:51,639
that's enough to bury all of London under more than half a kilometer of rock.

136
00:07:52,139 --> 00:07:56,985
The most recent super-eruption on Earth happened about 27,000 years ago,

137
00:07:56,985 --> 00:07:58,600
at Taupō in New Zealand.

138
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,406
Yellowstone is the famous one.

139
00:08:01,406 --> 00:08:09,401
The US Geological Survey puts the yearly chance of another giant eruption there at about one in 730,000.

140
00:08:09,401 --> 00:08:13,321
And it says there's no evidence that one is coming.

141
00:08:14,167 --> 00:08:15,394
But here's the twist.

142
00:08:15,394 --> 00:08:17,498
Something like this did happen once.

143
00:08:17,498 --> 00:08:18,725
Just not all at once.

144
00:08:19,225 --> 00:08:23,001
About 252 million years ago, in what's now Siberia,

145
00:08:23,001 --> 00:08:26,185
the ground cracked open, and kept erupting.

146
00:08:26,685 --> 00:08:33,139
The Siberian Traps released enough lava and magma to cover an area the size of the United States,

147
00:08:33,139 --> 00:08:34,270
a kilometer deep.

148
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:36,051
But it took its time.

149
00:08:36,051 --> 00:08:41,359
The big eruptions began about three hundred thousand years before the great extinction.

150
00:08:41,359 --> 00:08:45,874
And a third of the magma came in the five hundred thousand years after it.

151
00:08:46,374 --> 00:08:51,654
Scientists think its gases, including enormous amounts of carbon dioxide,

152
00:08:51,654 --> 00:08:54,547
helped drive the end-Permian extinction.

153
00:08:54,547 --> 00:08:55,704
By one estimate,

154
00:08:55,704 --> 00:09:01,997
more than 96 percent of ocean species and about 70 percent of land species disappeared.

155
00:09:02,497 --> 00:09:09,371
So the worst volcanic event in Earth's history wasn't thirteen hundred volcanoes erupting in a single day.

156
00:09:09,371 --> 00:09:14,236
It was one region erupting, on and off, for hundreds of thousands of years.

157
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:17,969
So could every volcano really erupt at once?

158
00:09:17,969 --> 00:09:19,143
Scientists say no.

159
00:09:19,643 --> 00:09:24,360
Each volcano is fed by its own local supply of magma, deep underground.

160
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,818
There's no switch that connects them.

161
00:09:26,818 --> 00:09:34,525
Science Focus magazine compared it to every bubble in a fizzy drink reaching the surface at exactly the same moment.

162
00:09:35,025 --> 00:09:40,127
On a normal day, a few dozen volcanoes are erupting somewhere on Earth.

163
00:09:40,127 --> 00:09:45,733
In March 2022, the Smithsonian's Global Volcanism Program counted forty-eight.

164
00:09:46,233 --> 00:09:49,788
And that's how volcanoes really shape our planet.

165
00:09:49,788 --> 00:09:50,296
Slowly.

166
00:09:50,296 --> 00:09:51,820
Eruption by eruption.

167
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:53,053
Island by island.

168
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:56,003
Which brings us back to Iwo Jima.

169
00:09:56,003 --> 00:10:00,974
One eruption, three weeks, and about ninety football fields of brand-new land.

170
00:10:01,474 --> 00:10:05,768
Volcanoes can chill the planet, ground every plane, and end an era.

171
00:10:05,768 --> 00:10:09,164
But they also build new ground, right in front of us.

172
00:10:09,664 --> 00:10:10,829
So what do you think?

173
00:10:10,829 --> 00:10:13,993
Will Iwo Jima's new beach still be there a year from now?

174
00:10:13,993 --> 00:10:15,325
Tell me in the comments.
